Usage examples:
- As init (PID 1): kernel cmdline … init=/sbin/sysx. It will mount /proc,/sys,/dev; fsck; mount -a; ip link set lo up; bring first NIC up; and start agetty on tty1. Tasks are auto-supervised with restart/backoff and logging to /var/log/sysx.log and /dev/console.
- As CLI from a shell (not PID 1):
  - sysx list
  - sysx start getty-tty1
  - sysx stop getty-tty1

Notes and limitations:
- No systemd/sysvinit, dbus, x11/xorg/xinit, elogind, or systemctl are used.
- Networking is intentionally minimal and daemon-free. For DHCP, run a client manually if desired after login.
- For real-world deployments, add signal handling for shutdown/reboot and clean unmount on poweroff.
- Ensure required binaries exist in the rootfs.

Below is a single-file, compilable Rust implementation of a minimal sysx init for Devuan that avoids systemd/sysvinit/dbus/X/elogind/systemctl and provides a simple CLI for listing/starting/stopping supervised tasks with restart policy and logging. It uses only standard userspace tools (mount, fsck, ip, getty) via direct exec/spawn and simple /proc supervision. Comments explain each step.

Build and usage:
- Build: cargo build --release
- Install: copy target/release/sysx to /sbin/sysx
- As PID 1: set kernel cmdline init=/sbin/sysx
- As CLI (on a running system where sysx is PID 1): run sysx list | start <task> | stop <task>

Notes and constraints:
- No systemd, sysvinit, dbus, X/xorg/x11, elogind, xinit, or systemctl are used.
- Uses /bin/busybox or standard tools if present: mount, fsck, ip, getty (or agetty). Adjust paths if needed.
- Networking is brought up with ip link/ip addr/ip route. Provide your interface name (e.g., eth0) via kernel cmdline sysx.iface=eth0 or it will try to auto-pick the first non-loopback.

